INT. COMMUNITY HOSPITAL, TRAUMA BAY - NIGHT

White light, steel. A gurney. Monitors beeping. A fan of gauze packets on a tray.

DR. SAMUEL ACHTERBERG, 40s, scrubs, surgical mask pulled under his chin, wipes his hands. NURSE LINH DAO, 30s, adjusts a monitor. A bleeding man, MR. TEAGUE, 50s, in a mechanic's coveralls, sits on the gurney, one hand clamped on his forearm.

Teague tries to stand. Achterberg and Dao push him back down together. Teague shoves free, swings an elbow. Dao ducks. Achterberg grabs the arm. The three of them scuffle against the gurney, a monitor lead pops loose, and the machine screams.

They freeze, panting. Teague's arm drips onto the floor. A long beat.

DR. ACHTERBERG
Mr. Teague, you are bleeding.

TEAGUE
I know I'm bleeding.

DR. ACHTERBERG
You have a lacerated radial artery. If you stand up, you will pass out.

TEAGUE
I don't have time to pass out.

NURSE DAO
Mr. Teague, please sit down.

TEAGUE
My daughter's waiting in the parking lot. She's eleven. She doesn't have a coat. She thinks I'm getting a sandwich.

DR. ACHTERBERG
We can send someone to get her.

TEAGUE
She won't go with a stranger.

NURSE DAO
She'll go with me. Tell me what she looks like.

TEAGUE
Blue backpack. Braids. Her name is Corinne.

NURSE DAO
Corinne. Blue backpack. Braids.

TEAGUE
She hates broccoli.

NURSE DAO
Noted.

DR. ACHTERBERG
Mr. Teague, I need your arm.

TEAGUE
You'll fix it?

DR. ACHTERBERG
I'll fix it.

Teague lies back. Dao reconnects the monitor lead. Achterberg unwraps a suture kit. The beep slows to a steady rhythm.
